  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Estimate the area under the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+3x+1$ over the interval $[1.5,3.5]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14221930/assets/ad1e85721eb2142f5cad422530aeed86c0919f34261d19e11e9f056cf355b387.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1.5)+f(2)+f(2.5)+f(3)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3.25$ | $3$ | $2.25$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3.25+3+2.25+1)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =9.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =4.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly decreasing* over the interval $[1.5,3.5],$ the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the actual area.",
